  "sport": "basketball",
  "title": "The shooting lap – five spots",
  "description": "Mark five shooting spots with cones just inside the three-point line: left corner, left wing, top, right wing and right corner. Players work in pairs at one basket – a shooter and a partner who rebounds and passes. The shooter starts in one corner and has to make a shot from each spot before moving on to the next. After two misses on the same spot the shooter moves on anyway, so the lap never gets stuck.\n\nThe rebounder catches the ball, make or miss, and passes it out to the shooter at the next spot. When the shooter has finished the lap the pair switch roles. With more pairs, put two pairs on a basket starting in opposite corners.\n\nProgressions: compete on who completes the lap in the fewest shots, put a clock on it, or move the cones out to the three-point line for experienced players. Easier: shorter range or only three spots.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Be ready before the ball arrives: hands up, knees bent and feet facing the basket."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "The same shooting motion from every spot – only your legs change the power."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Hold your follow-through and watch the ball all the way."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Rebounder: pass at chest height to the shooter so they can shoot straight away."
    }
  ],
